# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scrcpy-rt
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build outputs. The shipping binaries are RELEASE ASSETS, not tracked files.
build/
dist/
*.obj
*.lib
*.exe
*.dll
*.pdb
*.res
*.zip
*.tar.*
*.log
# ... except this one. It is a SHELL SCRIPT named lib.exe, because FFmpeg's makedef
# falls back to a hardcoded lib.exe when $AR is unset and looks for it on PATH.
# NOTE: gitignore has no trailing comments -- a `!pattern  # why` line silently matches
# nothing, which is how this exception was dead until it was checked with check-ignore.
!cross/bin/lib.exe

# Vendored upstream sources. Reconstruct them with scripts/fetch-*.sh -- the pinned tag and
# sha256 are in there. Redistributing somebody else's tree here would make this repo
# unreviewable and the patch count meaningless.
deps/

# Working notes, batch specifications and device-trip reports. These carry the project's
# history, not its product, and they are where the machine-specific strings live.
BATCH-*.md
reports/
STAGE-*-RESULTS.md
TRIP-*-RESULTS.md
STATE.md
RECON.md
RECIPE.md
notes/
CLAUDE.md

# ⚠ THIRD-PARTY PREBUILT BINARIES -- MUST NOT BE PUBLISHED.
# Somebody else's 2013 ARM32 builds, used here only as reference instruments: the old adb
# answered "can a Surface RT drive a modern phone?" and the old FFmpeg answered "can a Tegra 3
# decode H.264 at all?". Their licence and provenance are unknown, so they are not ours to
# redistribute. The README says what they were and where they came from.
adb-1.0.31-winarm32/
adb-1.0.31-winarm32.zip
ffmpeg2.1_ARM/
ffmpeg2.1_ARM.zip

# Staged copies of the sibling batches, which now live in the real repos. A duplicate that
# drifts from the original is worse than no copy.
new-repos/

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*~

# The scrub list names real identifiers -- never commit it.
.scrub-discriminators

# Frozen copies of a script, made so it can be edited while a long run is in flight.
# A killed run can leave one behind, and `git add -A` would otherwise stage it.
scripts/.*
